public int minCostClimbingStairs(int[] cost) {
    int n = cost.length;
    int[] dp = new int[n];
    dp[0] = cost[0];
    dp[1] = cost[1];
    for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2]) + cost[i];
    }
    return Math.min(dp[n - 1], dp[n - 2]);
}
```

Note: This solution uses dynamic programming to solve the problem. It maintains a `dp` array of size `n`, where `n` is the length of the input array `cost`. The `dp` array is initialized with the first two elements of the input array. For each element `i` in the input array, the minimum cost to reach position `i` is calculated by comparing the cost of reaching position `i` from either the previous element `i - 2` or the element before the previous `i - 1`. The result is stored in the `dp` array at position `i`. Finally, the minimum cost of reaching the end of the staircase is returned.

